<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1545">1545</a>–<a href="/years/1617">1617</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> from 1573 to 1617. A leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, he forced Protestants to convert or leave, refounded the University of Würzburg in 1582 and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliusspital">Juliusspital</a> hospital. His reign also saw large witch trials in the prince-bishopric.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-echter-von-mespelbrunn.jpg" alt="Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn" width="270" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julius_Echter_1586.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn was born on 18 March 1545 at Mespelbrunn Castle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spessart">Spessart</a>, the second son of the diplomat Peter III Echter. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a>, Pavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he became a licentiate of canon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>. Appointed a canon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> in 1554, he took up the office in 1567, became dean of the cathedral chapter in 1570, and in 1573, at twenty-eight and before his ordination as a priest, was made prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In his first ten years he tried to unite the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> with his bishopric. Allied with the Fulda chapter, the Hessian gentry and the Lutheran knights, he forced Prince-Abbot Balthasar von Dernbach to abdicate at Hammelburg in 1576 and was installed as administrator on condition that he tolerate the knights' Lutheran faith, a condition he had no intention of honouring in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>.</p><p>There he carried out a thorough ecclesiastical restoration, promoting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, who ran the refounded university when it opened on 2 January 1582 and supplied the see with priests and officials. He founded a seminary in 1589, banished Lutheran preachers, removed priests who would not keep the rules of their office, and admitted only Catholics as officials and teachers. Within three years about 100,000 people reportedly returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. He also built or renovated around 300 churches, besides rectories and schools, reduced taxes and improved the administration of justice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marienberg-fortress">Marienberg Fortress</a> on 9 September 1617, aged 72. His body was buried in Würzburg Cathedral, but breaking with the custom of heart burial at Ebrach Abbey he had his heart placed in the Neubaukirche, which he had built. After that church was destroyed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the heart was moved, and for the 400th anniversary of the university it was returned to the rebuilt church, now the university's great hall, in a two-ton monument.</p><p>The historian Dillinger counts him among the spearheads of Tridentine reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, for whom the fight against witches was part of an apocalyptic battle against evil. His most lasting monument after the university is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliusspital">Juliusspital</a>, founded with the endowment of the abandoned monastery of Heiligenthal. St John's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, holds 20 volumes from his library, taken during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn</b> refounded the university in 1582, built the Juliusspital and drove a forceful Counter-Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg">Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mespelbrunn, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.92,9.28&amp;year=1591" aria-label="Mespelbrunn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5509 5822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5509 5822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.92,9.28&amp;year=1591">What was here in 1591? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
